WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:06.130
The full It: Chapter Two trailer debuted July
18th, 2019 at San Diego Comic-Con, promising

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:11.120
a thrilling conclusion to director Andy Muschietti's
adaptation of Stephen King's terrifying novel.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.960
The trailer suggests a devout and appropriate
faithfulness to the flick's source material.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.640
Let's have a look at the chilling details.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.580
The trailer opens with narration from Mike,
who unlike the rest of the now-adult Losers

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.580
stayed in Derry, Maine.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.220
"The farther away, the hazier it all gets.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.440
But me, I never left.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:31.400
I remember all of it."

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.960
Then there's a shot of the sewers, where a
torrent of water rolls through a tunnel, ensaring

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:36.820
a young victim.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.720
This scene doesn't seem to be from the It
novel; it instead looks like a nod to another

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:45.000
film adaptation of a King classic: The 1980
Stanley Kubrick film The Shining.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.770
The famous trailer for that film uses a static
shot from the movie of some unassuming elevator

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:54.060
doors, but as the film's title and credits
pop up onscreen, massive volume of blood gushes

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.780
out even as the doors stay closed.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.860
It's unlikely in the extreme that Muschietti
would have included such a similar shot by

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:00.360
coincidence.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.020
In King's novel, the number seven is a sort
of mystical totem that helps to empower the

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.740
Losers to take on a malevolent being from
beyond time and space.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.310
When Mike calls the adult Losers back to Derry,
however, their chances against Pennywise are

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.800
immediately dealt a blow by the fact that
their number is reduced to six.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.200
Stan Uris, the most logical of the bunch,
remembers just a bit too much about that long-ago

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.340
summer upon receiving Mike's invitation.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.600
Just minutes after receiving Mike's call,
Stan commits suicide.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.660
It: Chapter Two will clearly carry over this
plot point from the book; in every single

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:33.940
one of the trailer's shots that feature the
entire adult Losers' Club, Stan isn't there.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.940
In one of the new trailer's most harrowing
sequences, James McAvoy's Bill is seen pursuing

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.689
a young boy through a carnival funhouse.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:45.009
He loses the youngster in the hall of mirrors,
finally managing to catch up to him, but they're

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:46.500
separated by thick glass.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.740
In a horrible echo of his brother Georgie's
fate in the opening of the first film, Pennywise

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.000
materializes to menace the child as a helpless
Billy frantically kicks at the glass.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.120
Funhouses are meant to be a little scary,
sure, but those gently swaying pendulums would

00:01:59.139 --> 00:02:02.319
make any child in their right mind run screaming
from the attraction.

00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:06.209
They bear more than a passing resemblance
to Pennywise himself, and while some of them

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:08.940
are smiling, others sport a look of wide-eyed,
abject terror.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.030
It's a visual signifier that, as Bill enters
the funhouse, he's entering the monster's

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:13.520
domain.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.180
In a blink-and-you'll-miss-it moment, an unidentified
man is dragged through the halls of what appears

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.800
to be a mental institution.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.410
Oblivious to the staff member trying to place
him into a chokehold, he's fixated with rapturous

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.780
joy on a red balloon Pennywise's calling card
floating outside.

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.760
As fans of the novel are well aware, this
is no random patient.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:35.380
This is the adult Henry Bowers, who as a child
terrorized the Losers' Club and fell under

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.500
the sway of Pennywise, causing him to kill
his abusive father.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:39.540
"That's right!

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.840
And you will, too, Henry!

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.300
Make it a wonderful day!

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:45.440
Kill him!"

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.340
In King's novel, Henry is committed to Juniper
Hill asylum for his crime, where he spends

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.680
27 years obsessing over his encounter with
forces beyond his comprehension.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.820
When Pennywise returns, Bowers escapes, with
an assist from the shapeshifting entity, and

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.300
tries to kill the Losers.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.720
When the Losers reconvene in the book, they
decide to split up to individually explore

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:05.400
old haunts.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.920
Of course, Pennywise takes this opportunity
to taunt them all, assaulting them with reality-warping

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.580
experiences that turn the most innocent of
their formative memories on their heads.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:17.780
One of the most memorable of these sequences
involves Richie Tozier, who pays a visit to

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.120
the town's park with its hilariously tacky
statue of Paul Bunyan.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.540
Richie's solo adventure is briefly depicted
in the trailer, as he sees Pennywise gliding

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.920
along with a bundle of balloons above that
very statue.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:32.360
In the book, Henry Bowers' gang once chased
him to that exact spot, where the Bunyan statue

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.020
came to life, chasing him down with its tremendous
ax.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.060
The statue morphs into a bizarre version of
Pennywise and proceeds to warn him of his

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:39.860
impending doom.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:40.860
"All right, Rich.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:41.940
What are you afraid of?"

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:46.280
"Clowns."

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.320
In one shot near the trailer's end, Mike is
in a crude tent, as the world appears to be

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.040
shaking itself to pieces around him.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.780
He doesn't appear frightened, though.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.080
He seems almost joyous.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.080
This may actually be exactly what's happening
in this shot, as it appears to be a depiction

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.880
of a ritual the Losers engaged in as children
in the novel.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.800
This could be a weird kind of flashback, or
the filmmakers may have chosen to move it

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:08.560
to the present day.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.500
It involves a "Smoke-Hole," a construct built
for a Native American ritual which Ben discovers

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.160
and which is meant to bring enlightenment.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:20.340
Sitting in an enclosure while burning a dry,
smoky fire, the Losers all experience visions,

00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:24.099
one of which reveals that Pennywise came to
Earth from another dimension millennia ago.

00:04:24.099 --> 00:04:28.300
It's impact, similar to that of a small asteroid,
created the deep caverns under Derry where

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.480
Pennywise makes its home.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.599
The Losers are finally able to kill It for
good by engaging in the Ritual of Chüd, the

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:38.770
details of which were revealed to Bill by
Maturin, a turtle-shaped eternal force of

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.500
light diametrically opposed to It.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.560
The Losers must use their wit, knowledge,
and love for each other to essentially will

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:45.560
It into submission.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.760
In the novel, it's a battle that takes place
purely in the psychic realm, where belief

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.800
and purity of intention are capable of defeating
even the most malevolent forces.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.900
In the trailer, the surviving Losers certainly
seem to be about to engage in some sort of

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.400
ritual.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.560
Screenwriter Gary Dauberman has said that
a form of the Ritual of Chüd will be featured

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.770
in It: Chapter Two, describing his writing
of the sequence as:

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.980
"...trying to find the most focused, accessible
way into some of more metaphysical aspects

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:10.480
of that book."

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.920
It'll be interesting to see how he and Muschietti
tackled one of the most abstract climaxes

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.420
in all of horror literature, but they have
our confidence, and King's as well.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.180
In a tweet, the author said simply,

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.260
"Looking forward to It: Chapter Two?

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.040
You should be.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.500
I've seen it, and it's terrific."

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.480
We'll know if we agree when the flick hits
screens September 6th.

